---
/**
 * The Labour Code wage rule, worked through with the engine's own numbers. Every table is computed
 * at build time from the same functions the calculator runs.
 */
import DocLayout from '../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import { bracketPath } from '../lib/brackets';
import { labourCodeData } from '../lib/explainers';
import { formatPercent, formatRupees } from '../lib/format';

const data = labourCodeData();
const low = data.rows.find((r) => r.basicPercent === data.lowBasicPercent)!;
const full = data.rows.find((r) => r.basicPercent === 50)!;
const lowest = data.rows[0];
const twelve = data.gratuityShift.find((r) => r.lpa === data.exampleLpa)!;
const esiTop = data.esi.filter((r) => r.covered).at(-1)!;
const understatement = 1 - twelve.onBasic / twelve.onWages;
const lakh = (ctc: number) => `₹${(ctc / 1_00_000).toFixed(2).replace(/\.?0+$/, '')} lakh`;
---

<DocLayout
  title="The Labour Code 50% wage rule, worked out"
  description="Since 21 November 2025, PF, ESI and gratuity are worked out on Labour Code wages. What the 50% rule adds back, why a low Basic no longer lowers them, and who it brings into ESI."
  updated="2026-10-09"
  ogImage="labour-code-wages"
  about={['Labour Codes', 'Code on Wages 2019', 'Provident fund', 'Gratuity', 'ESI']}
>
  <p>
    The four Labour Codes came into force across India on 21 November 2025. They give PF, ESI and
    gratuity one shared definition of <strong>wages</strong>, and with it a rule: if your allowances
    and the other payments left out of wages come to more than half of your total pay, the excess is
    counted as wages anyway.
  </p>
  <p>
    That rule is often summarised as "Basic must be at least 50% of CTC". It says nothing of the
    sort. Your employer can still pay a small Basic and a large special allowance, and your payslip
    need not change. What changes is the figure PF, ESI and gratuity are calculated on.
  </p>

  <h2>How the rule works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Wages</strong> start as Basic pay, dearness allowance and retaining allowance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Excluded payments</strong> are the things the definition leaves out: HRA, conveyance,
      travel concessions, your employer's PF and pension contributions, statutory bonus, overtime
      and commission. The Ministry of Labour also treats special allowance and other general
      allowances this way.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Total remuneration</strong> is the two together. Gratuity and ESI are not part of it.
    </li>
    <li>
      If excluded payments are more than <strong>half of total remuneration</strong>, the excess is
      added to wages.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    At {data.exampleLpa} LPA with Basic at {data.lowBasicPercent}% of CTC, Basic is{' '}
    {formatRupees(low.basicMonthly)} a month and total remuneration is{' '}
    {formatRupees(low.remuneration)}. The excluded payments come to{' '}
    {formatRupees(low.excludedPayments)}, which is {formatPercent(low.excludedShare)} of the total.
    Half of the total is {formatRupees(low.remuneration / 2)}, so {formatRupees(low.addBack)} is
    added back and wages for PF, ESI and gratuity become{' '}
    <strong>{formatRupees(low.wages)} a month</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>A lower Basic no longer means lower PF or gratuity</h2>
  <p>
    The same {data.exampleLpa} LPA CTC with Basic set at different shares, special allowance taking
    up the rest. Below half of CTC the rule pulls wages back to the same level every time.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Basic</th>
          <th scope="col">Excluded share</th>
          <th scope="col">Added back</th>
          <th scope="col">Wages</th>
          <th scope="col">Gratuity a year</th>
          <th scope="col">PF on full wages</th>
          <th scope="col">In hand a month</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.rows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              {r.basicPercent}%
              <span class="block text-xs text-ink-3">{formatRupees(r.basicMonthly)}</span>
            </td>
            <td>{formatPercent(r.excludedShare)}</td>
            <td>{r.addBack > 0 ? formatRupees(r.addBack) : 'Nothing'}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.wages)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.gratuityYearly)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.fullPfMonthly)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.takeHome)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Monthly figures except gratuity. "PF on full wages" is your own 12% if your employer contributes
    on full wages rather than up to the wage ceiling. In-hand assumes PF at the ceiling, gratuity
    and employer PF inside CTC, and the cheaper tax regime.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whether Basic is {lowest.basicPercent}% or {data.rows[2].basicPercent}% of CTC, wages come out
    at {formatRupees(low.wages)} and monthly in-hand at {formatRupees(low.takeHome)}. With Basic at
    half of CTC the structure passes the test on its own, nothing is added back, and in-hand is{' '}
    {formatRupees(full.takeHome)}. Restructuring pay to keep Basic low no longer saves anything on
    PF or gratuity.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does to gratuity</h2>
  <p>
    Gratuity is 15/26 of a month's wages for each year of service, and since November 2025 those are
    Labour Code wages. Many calculators still use Basic alone. With Basic at {data.lowBasicPercent}%
    of CTC, here is one year's gratuity both ways.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">CTC</th>
          <th scope="col">Basic a month</th>
          <th scope="col">Wages a month</th>
          <th scope="col">A year's gratuity on Basic</th>
          <th scope="col">On Labour Code wages</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.gratuityShift.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <a href={bracketPath(r.lpa)}>{r.lpa} LPA</a>
            </td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.basicMonthly)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.wages)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.onBasic)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.onWages)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Using Basic alone understates gratuity by {formatPercent(understatement, 0)} for this structure.
    The <a href="/gratuity-calculator">gratuity guide</a> covers the formula, the one-year rule for
    fixed-term staff and the ₹20 lakh maximum.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it brings into ESI</h2>
  <p>
    ESI applies when wages are no more than {formatRupees(data.esiCeiling)} a month. That ceiling is
    now tested on Labour Code wages, not gross pay, and for most salaried people wages are only
    about half of gross. So ESI reaches much further up the pay scale than it used to.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">CTC</th>
          <th scope="col">Gross pay a month</th>
          <th scope="col">Wages a month</th>
          <th scope="col">ESI</th>
          <th scope="col">Your ESI a month</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.esi.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <a href={bracketPath(r.lpa)}>{r.lpa} LPA</a>
            </td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.grossMonthly)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.wages)}</td>
            <td>{r.covered ? 'Applies' : 'Does not apply'}</td>
            <td>
              {r.covered ? formatRupees(r.employeeMonthly, r.employeeMonthly % 1 !== 0) : 'None'}
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    With Basic at half of CTC, ESI applies up to a CTC of about {lakh(data.esiUpTo)}. At{' '}
    {esiTop.lpa} LPA your gross pay is {formatRupees(esiTop.grossMonthly)} a month and ESI still
    applies. You pay 0.75% of wages and your employer 3.25%, and in return you get ESI medical
    cover. Not every employer has caught up with this, so check your payslip if you are in this
    range.
  </p>

  <h2>The two choices we had to make</h2>
  <p>The rule leaves two questions open, and the answers change the numbers:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>What counts as total remuneration.</strong> We follow the Ministry of Labour's FAQs of
      16 March 2026: employer PF and pension contributions are included, gratuity and ESI are not.
      An earlier FAQ of 30 December 2025 included gratuity in a worked example; the later, more
      specific answer is used.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Special allowance.</strong> Neither list names it. The Ministry tests every payment
      other than Basic, DA and retaining allowance against the 50% limit, and that is our default.
      Read literally, the statute could make such allowances wages in full, which raises gratuity
      and can change ESI. The calculator's advanced mode has a switch for that reading.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to check in your offer letter</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Whether PF is on the wage ceiling or on full wages. The difference is large; see the{' '}
      <a href="/provident-fund">provident fund guide</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Whether employer PF and gratuity are inside your CTC. If they are, any increase in wages comes
      out of the rest of your CTC, usually special allowance.
    </li>
    <li>
      Whether Basic is below half of CTC. If it is, your PF, gratuity and ESI should still be worked
      out on the higher Labour Code wages.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Try it with your own structure</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href={`/?m=advanced&b=${data.lowBasicPercent}p`}>calculator in advanced mode</a> takes
    your actual Basic, HRA and allowances, applies the rule, and tells you when something has been
    added back.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sources: Code on Wages, 2019, s.2(y), and Code on Social Security, 2020, s.2(88), in force from
    21 November 2025; Ministry of Labour and Employment FAQs of 30 December 2025 and Additional FAQs
    of 16 March 2026. <a href="/how-it-works">Full method</a>.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
